  "faqs": [
    {
      "question": "Can I buy a car right after Chapter 7?",
      "answer": "Yes, you can apply for an auto loan immediately after the court grants your Chapter 7 discharge. The discharge order is typically entered 60 to 90 days after your meeting of creditors, eliminating personal liability on eligible debts and allowing you to take on new financing without trustee oversight."
    },
    {
      "question": "Does CarMax work with Chapter 13?",
      "answer": "Any dealership or lender financing a borrower in an active Chapter 13 case requires prior approval from the bankruptcy trustee or court under 11 U.S.C. § 1305. Approval to incur new debt depends on whether your trustee or the bankruptcy court approves the financing terms."
    },
    {
      "question": "Should I reaffirm my car loan?",
      "answer": "Reaffirming an auto loan under 11 U.S.C. § 524 makes you legally responsible for the debt even after bankruptcy discharge. If you keep the car and reaffirm, you remain personally liable on the debt and risk repossession if you default later, so evaluate whether your budget realistically supports the ongoing payments."
    },
    {
      "question": "Will I get a high interest rate after bankruptcy?",
      "answer": "Borrowers with recent bankruptcies generally receive higher interest rates due to lower credit scores. A 2021 CFPB study showed subprime borrowers at finance companies and buy-here-pay-here lots averaged 15 to 20 percent rates, while bank subprime loans averaged roughly 10 percent, making rate shopping vital."
    },
    {
      "question": "Can I keep my car in Chapter 7?",
      "answer": "You can keep your car in Chapter 7 if your equity is exempt and you choose an approved statutory path: reaffirming the loan under 11 U.S.C. § 524 or redeeming the vehicle under 11 U.S.C. § 722 by paying the allowed secured claim in a lump sum."
    }
